{
  // The repo is **CommonJS**, and that is a requirement rather than a
  // preference: a downstream build system consumes this code and accepts
  // `__dirname` but not `import.meta`. Under CommonJS `import.meta` is a syntax
  // error rather than a diagnostic — which is why it was already banned
  // repo-wide (`tools/style.ts`) while this was still ESM, and why the ban now
  // describes what the compiler would do anyway.
  //
  // Two consequences worth knowing before writing code here:
  //
  //   - `__dirname` and `__filename` exist. Paths resolve from them rather than
  //     from the working directory or `process.argv[1]`.
  //   - **Top-level `await` is illegal everywhere**, not just in `bin/`. The
  //     style checker enforces that; entrypoints use `void run().then(…)`.
  //
  // `Node16` rather than plain `CommonJS` because it is the resolver that reads
  // `exports` maps, and this package publishes one: `.`, `./protocol`,
  // `./workflow`, `./activity`. Nothing in this tree imports itself by package
  // name — the dashboard did, and it is gone — so the map is checked here only
  // in the sense that this is the resolution mode a consumer will be using. The
  // older resolver cannot see a subpath at all, which would make the published
  // surface untestable from any angle.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "Node16",
    "moduleResolution": "Node16",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "strict": true,
    "noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "types": ["node", "jasmine"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "spec/**/*.ts", "bin/**/*.ts"]
}
